2026-06-30 15:30:03,846 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-0b504ce1-e374-4f95-ac34-e172cb7ded09', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nTS_ATP_ELU_1.raw\nTS_ATP_ELU_2.raw\nTS_ATP_ELU_3.raw\nTS_ATP_ELU_4.raw\nTS_ATP_INP_1.raw\nTS_ATP_INP_2.raw\nTS_ATP_INP_3.raw\nTS_ATP_INP_4.raw\nTS_FLP_ELU_1.raw\nTS_FLP_ELU_2.raw\nTS_FLP_ELU_3.raw\nTS_FLP_ELU_4.raw\nTS_FLP_INP_1.raw\nTS_FLP_INP_2.raw\nTS_FLP_INP_3.raw\nTS_FLP_INP_4.raw\nTS_ILE_ELU_1.raw\nTS_ILE_ELU_2.raw\nTS_ILE_ELU_3.raw\nTS_ILE_ELU_4.raw\nTS_ILE_INP_1.raw\nTS_ILE_INP_2.raw\nTS_ILE_INP_3.raw\nTS_ILE_INP_4.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nMitochondrial translocase blocking ATP5MG-tFT fusion protein interactome in human embryonic kidney (HEK) 293 cells\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe experiment uses HEK-293 Flp-In T-Rex cells to express fusion protein that combines ATP synthase subunit ATP5MG with a tandem fluorescent protein timer (tFT). The tFT tag combines mCherry and super folder GFP (sfGFP) fluorescent proteins. An exceptionally stable structure of sfGFP interferes with its import into mitochondria. Thus, ATP5MG-tFT fusion stalls during translocation into mitochondria. We identify the proteome of cells after 24 hours of fusion protein expression. As a control, we use cells that express tFT fusion not directed to mitochondria (ILE-Deg-tFT) or cells that did not express fusion protein. Next, we use chemical crosslinking and GFP-Trap-based immunoprecipitation to define interaction partner proteins of ATP5MG-tFT and ILE-Deg-tFT fusions.\n\n[METHODS]\nHEK-293 Flp-In T-Rex cells and their derivatives expressing ATP5MG-tFT or ILE-Deg-tFT were grown in high glucose DMEM medium supplemented with 10% FBS and Penicillin/Streptomycin mix. During the final 24 hours, growth media was supplemented with 1 µg/ml of tetracycline to induce fusion protein synthesis. Cells were harvested in PBS. Pellets of five million cells were crosslinked by incubation in 0,5 ml of 0,5% formaldehyde in PBS for 30 min at room temperature (RT). Next, cell pellets were incubated with 1,25 M glycine solution in PBS for 10 minutes at RT to quench any remaining formaldehyde. After glycine treatment, cells were washed three times with PBS. Three million cells were lysed in 200 µL of lysis buffer (10 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.5, 150 mM NaCl, 0.5 mM EDTA, 0.5% NP-40, protease inhibitor cocktail) and incubated on ice for 30 min. The lysates were spun at 20,000 g for 10 min to eliminate insoluble material, and protein concentration in the supernatants was measured. Samples were diluted with wash buffer (10 mM Tris-HCl pH=7.5, 150 mM NaCl, 0.5 mM EDTA, protease inhibitor cocktail) to adjust protein concentration to 0,3 µg/µg and reduce detergent concentration by approximately three-fold. An equal amount of protein (150 µg) for each sample was used for immunoprecipitation with 10 µL of GFP-Trap Agarose beads slurry (ChromoTek) (supplemented with 40 µL of empty agarose beads to facilitate handling), while 15 µg of each sample was saved and processed as INPUT fractions. The beads were washed with the wash buffer three times and then incubated with trypsin (sequencing grade from Promega, 1 µg) in 50 mM Tris-HCL buffer pH 8.2 containing 5 mM TCEP and 10 mM iodoacetamide overnight at 37 °C. Proteins in the INPUT fractions were subjected to chloroform/methanol precipitation. Protein pellets were resuspended in 50 mM Tris-HCL buffer pH 8.2 containing 5 mM TCEP, 10 mM iodoacetamide. Trypsin (sequencing grade from Promega, 0,5 µg) was added, and the samples were incubated overnight at 37 °C. Proteolysis reactions were quenched by the addition of trifluoroacetic acid (TFA, to the final conc. 1%), and the samples were centrifuged at 12,000 x g for 3 min at RT, to pellet undigested proteins. Tryptic peptides were desalted with the use of AttractSPE™ Disks Bio C18 (Affinisep). Prior to LC-MS measurement, the peptide fractions were resuspended in 0.1% TFA, 2% acetonitrile in water. Chromatographic separation was performed on an Easy-Spray Acclaim PepMap column 50 cm × 75 µm inner diameter (Thermo Fisher Scientific) at 45 °C by applying a 135 min acetonitrile gradients in 0.1% aqueous formic acid at a flow rate of 300 nl/min. An UltiMate 3000 nano-LC system was coupled to a Q Exactive HF-X mass spectrometer via an easy-spray source (all Thermo Fisher Scientific). The Q Exactive HF-X was operated in data-dependent mode with survey scans acquired at a resolution of 60,000 at m/z 200. Up to 12 of the most abundant isotope patterns with charges z=2-6 from the survey scan were selected with an isolation window of 1.3 m/z and fragmented by higher-energy collision dissociation (HCD) with a normalized collision energy of 27, while the dynamic exclusion was set to 30 s. The maximum ion injection times for the survey scan and the MS/MS scans (acquired with a resolution of 15,000 at m/z 200) were 45 and 22 ms, respectively. The ion target value for MS was set to 3e6 and for MS/MS to 1e5, and the intensity threshold for MS/MS was set to 8e2.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
